.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt BANAN 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm banan
.Nd a simple queued job runner
.Pp
.Po
.Nm banan
means
.Do one by one Dc in Basque.
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl d | Fl -debug
.Op Fl -timeout Ar duration
.Op Fl -queued-pickup Ar duration
.Op Fl -remind-every Ar duration
.Op Fl -remind-banner Ar file
.Op Fl -log-dir Ar directory
.Op Fl e | Fl -environment Ar NAME=value
.Fl -
.Ar command
.Op Ar argument ...
.Nm
.Fl h | Fl -help
.Nm
.Fl -man
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility executes a
.Ar command
right away or, if another instance of that command is already being run
using
.Nm ,
waits for it to complete first, so that commands are executed one by one.
.Pp
The command is identified by the name of its executable:
for the command line
.Dq bash -c "echo 123"
the identification name is
.Dq bash .
.Pp
Instances of
.Nm
coordinate with each other through a log file named after the identification
name with the file extension removed, with the
.Pa .log
suffix added:
.Pa bash.log
for
.Pa bash ,
.Pa script.log
for
.Pa script.sh .
The log is locked using the operating system's file locking mechanisms while
it is being read or written.
.Pp
Everything following the
.Fl -
argument is the command line to execute.
The command and each of its arguments are separate words, as with
.Xr env 1
or
.Xr nice 1 :
a quoted command line such as
.Dq "echo a b c"
is taken to be the name of a single executable, and is not found.
The standard output of the command is passed through unchanged.
The standard error of the command is written to the standard output as well,
and is also saved in the log if the command fails.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl d , Fl -debug
Show debugging output, on the standard error: locking and
unlocking of the log file, the records read from it, and the decisions
taken because of them.
.It Fl e , Fl -environment Ar NAME=value
Set an environment variable for the command, in addition to the environment
.Nm
itself has been run with.
May be given multiple times.
.It Fl -log-dir Ar directory
Create the log file in the
.Ar directory .
The default is the current directory.
.It Fl -queued-pickup Ar duration
Allow the next queued command to start running if the previously queued command
is slow to pick up its turn, that is if this much time has passed
since the previous command completed and the queued command has not started yet.
The default is
.Li 15s .
.It Fl -remind-banner Ar file
Show the contents of the
.Ar file
instead of the standard message while waiting.
The file is a Go
.Lk https://pkg.go.dev/html/template html/template
template that is executed against the running instance, so
.Li {{.Command}}
and
.Li {{.PID}}
are the command being waited for and the process identification
number of this instance.
.It Fl -remind-every Ar duration
Remind that the command is still waiting to be executed, by showing the
banner, this often.
The reminder is shown when the number of seconds since the Unix epoch is a multiple
of the whole number of seconds in the
.Ar duration ,
so anything shorter than a second is taken to be a second.
The default is
.Li 5s .
.It Fl -timeout Ar duration
Period after which a command that has been started and has not completed is
considered timed out: other instances stop waiting for it.
The default is
.Li 30m .
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Show the usage message.
.It Fl -man
Show this manual page, or, if neither the
.Xr mandoc 1
nor the
.Xr man 1
utility is available, the usage message.
.El
.Pp
.Sh LOG FORMAT
The log is a file of JSON objects, one per line, appended in the order the
events have happened:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"time":"2024-Apr-09 12:12:56 UTC","state":"Processing","pid":45772}
{"time":"2024-Apr-09 12:12:56 UTC","state":"Done","pid":45772,"wall_clock_time":"2.00s"}
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Dq state
field is one of:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Sy Processing
the instance has started running its command.
.It Sy Queued
the instance is waiting for another instance's command to complete.
.It Sy Done
the command has completed.
The record also has the
.Dq exit_code ,
the
.Dq wall_clock_time ,
and, if the command failed, the
.Dq error_message
fields.
.It Sy Killed
an instance that had been waited upon has been found not running anymore
without having recorded the completion.
.El
.Pp
Instances are served in the order they have joined the queue.
A
.Sy Queued
record older than four hours is disregarded.
.Sh SIGNALS
Upon receiving
.Dv SIGHUP ,
.Dv SIGINT ,
.Dv SIGQUIT ,
or
.Dv SIGTERM ,
.Nm
kills the command it is running, records it as
.Sy Done ,
and exits with the exit status 1.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
The command inherits the environment of
.Nm .
Additional variables can be set with the
.Fl e
option.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Pa ./ Ns Ar name Ns Pa .log
the log file, where
.Ar name
is the name of the command's executable without the extension.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits with the exit status of the command that has been run.
If the command could not be started, an error message is printed to the standard error
and the exit status is 127 if the command was not found, and 126 if it was found but
could not be executed, same as in a shell.
If the log cannot be read or written, or on a usage error, it exits with a
non-zero status.
.Pp
The
.Fl -man
option exits with 0, or with 2 if it has shown the usage message instead
of the manual page.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Run three instances of a command at the same time; they are executed
one after another:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ banan -- bash -c 'echo running; sleep 2' &
$ banan -- bash -c 'echo running; sleep 2' &
$ banan -- bash -c 'echo running; sleep 2'
.Ed
.Pp
Show the log afterwards:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ cat bash.log
.Ed
.Pp
Keep the log in a dedicated directory, consider the command timed out
after ten minutes, and let the next in line start if the previous
one is slow to pick up for more than five seconds:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ banan --log-dir /var/tmp --timeout 10m --queued-pickup 5s -- make deploy
.Ed
.Pp
Set an environment variable for the command:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ banan -e GOFLAGS=-count=2 -- go test ./...
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr flock 1 ,
.Xr lockf 1
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Peter Vypov <peter.vypov@gmail.com>
